WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.510 --> 00:00:03.130
In the '90s, Helen Hunt was everywhere.

00:00:03.130 --> 00:00:07.630
She starred in the long-running sitcom Mad
About You, and Hollywood was, well, mad about

00:00:07.630 --> 00:00:08.630
her.

00:00:08.630 --> 00:00:12.490
But since the show's cancellation in 1999,
we've heard less and less from the actress.

00:00:12.490 --> 00:00:16.090
Here's what Helen Hunt's been up to since
receding from the spotlight.

00:00:16.090 --> 00:00:17.330
Highs and lows

00:00:17.330 --> 00:00:21.060
It isn't like Hunt has stopped making movies
since her prime Mad About You days.

00:00:21.060 --> 00:00:24.090
It's just that, with a few exceptions, you
may not have heard about them.

00:00:24.090 --> 00:00:28.361
At the height of her career, Hunt was starring
in films like 1997's As Good As It Gets, an

00:00:28.361 --> 00:00:32.540
award-gobbling dramedy that pulled in almost
$315 million dollars worldwide.

00:00:32.540 --> 00:00:36.600
"Give me some sign you understand, or leave
now.

00:00:39.840 --> 00:00:41.480
Do you understand me?"

00:00:41.480 --> 00:00:46.730
A year before that, she was in Twister, a
disaster epic that grossed almost 500 million.

00:00:46.730 --> 00:00:51.519
Right after Mad About You ended, she starred
in 2000's What Women Want and Cast Away, which

00:00:51.519 --> 00:00:53.109
were both smash hits worldwide.

00:00:53.109 --> 00:00:54.190
"Thank you.

00:00:54.190 --> 00:00:55.800
I appreciate that."

00:00:55.800 --> 00:00:59.440
But then came starring roles in a sporadic
string of smaller films that failed to impress

00:00:59.440 --> 00:01:04.550
moviegoers or critics, such as Woody Allen's
The Curse of the Jade Scorpion; A Good Woman,

00:01:04.550 --> 00:01:09.300
an Oscar Wilde adaptation co-starring Scarlett
Johansson; and Emilio Estevez's widely panned

00:01:09.300 --> 00:01:12.400
Bobby, which centered on the day Robert Kennedy
was assassinated.

00:01:12.400 --> 00:01:17.400
In 2007, Hunt took her first shot at writing,
directing and starring in a film with Then

00:01:17.400 --> 00:01:20.480
She Found Me, an adaptation of the Elinor
Lipman novel.

00:01:20.480 --> 00:01:24.290
The film received mixed reviews, a limited
release, and didn't stir up any award-season

00:01:24.290 --> 00:01:28.270
buzz, but Hunt still returned to the director's
chair several times in the decade that followed

00:01:28.270 --> 00:01:33.070
— which is one reason fans haven't seen
much of her on-screen lately.

00:01:33.070 --> 00:01:34.270
Behind the camera

00:01:34.270 --> 00:01:38.630
As Variety recaps, Hunt started by directing
several episodes of Mad About You, but didn't

00:01:38.630 --> 00:01:42.320
return to directing until 2007, with Then
She Found Me.

00:01:42.320 --> 00:01:46.550
After another long break, she started directing
in earnest, focusing mostly on television.

00:01:46.550 --> 00:01:51.750
She's overseen two episodes of ABC's primetime
soap Revenge and one episode of NBC's breakout

00:01:51.750 --> 00:01:56.680
hit This Is Us, just to name two of the half-dozen
shows she's worked on in recent years.

00:01:56.680 --> 00:02:01.470
She also wrote and directed a second movie,
2014's surfing comedy Ride, which received

00:02:01.470 --> 00:02:02.470
mixed reviews.

00:02:02.470 --> 00:02:07.040
Many critics noted that Hunt's acting chops
still outshine her directorial prowess, even

00:02:07.040 --> 00:02:11.290
as recently as 2012, when she was nominated
for a Best Actress Oscar for The Sessions.

00:02:11.290 --> 00:02:12.390
"I'll have a chardonnay."

00:02:12.390 --> 00:02:13.390
"Me too."

00:02:13.390 --> 00:02:14.620
"And a glass of red wine."

00:02:14.620 --> 00:02:15.670
"May I see some ident—"

00:02:15.670 --> 00:02:16.670
"They're for me."

00:02:16.670 --> 00:02:17.240
"All of them?"

00:02:17.240 --> 00:02:18.740
"It's a stressful time."

00:02:19.170 --> 00:02:20.160
Return to TV

00:02:20.170 --> 00:02:25.889
In 2017, Hunt joined the Fox show Shots Fired,
a ten-episode miniseries about a black police

00:02:25.889 --> 00:02:30.010
officer in North Carolina who kills an unarmed
white man during a traffic stop.

00:02:30.010 --> 00:02:33.469
The investigation into the incident reveals
that the police are ignoring the murder of

00:02:33.469 --> 00:02:34.469
a black teenager.

00:02:34.469 --> 00:02:38.530
Hunt, who plays the governor of North Carolina,
told the Television Critics Association how

00:02:38.530 --> 00:02:43.610
great she felt about the incredibly timely
and topical show, calling the cast "amazing."

00:02:43.610 --> 00:02:48.299
Shots Fired is Hunt's first small-screen acting
gig in more than a decade, but 2017 also marks

00:02:48.299 --> 00:02:52.920
Hunt's return to film, with a part in Louis
C.K.'s controversial I Love You, Daddy.

00:02:52.920 --> 00:02:55.319
"China wants to come live with me for the
rest of her senior year."

00:02:55.319 --> 00:02:56.740
"Of course she wants to come live with you.

00:02:56.740 --> 00:03:00.470
You have a giant apartment, and you have the
Hamptons, and the plane."

00:03:00.470 --> 00:03:02.159
"None of that is my fault."

00:03:02.159 --> 00:03:03.959
Post-Oscar slowdown

00:03:03.959 --> 00:03:07.379
In an interview with The Daily Beast, Hunt
said that a big reason she shifted into low

00:03:07.379 --> 00:03:12.219
gear after her late '90s boom was waning studio
interest in the kind of movies that made her

00:03:12.219 --> 00:03:16.950
a star and earned her an Oscar — the dreaded
"dramedy," which Hunt calls her "least favorite

00:03:16.950 --> 00:03:17.950
word", saying,

00:03:17.950 --> 00:03:21.629
"When I wasn't getting acting jobs all the
time that I liked, I was writing and writing

00:03:21.629 --> 00:03:22.639
and writing.

00:03:22.639 --> 00:03:23.680
Ten years of that."

00:03:23.680 --> 00:03:27.599
This led to Then She Found Me, which Hunt
admits was poorly timed, saying,

00:03:27.599 --> 00:03:31.680
"I'd just been in the last big wave of movies
about people talking to each other and trying

00:03:31.680 --> 00:03:35.969
to love each other, so as that was shrinking,
I was trying to make one of those movies."

00:03:35.969 --> 00:03:39.599
In other words: both Hunt and the studios
got sick of the kind of movies she was making

00:03:39.599 --> 00:03:44.099
at the exact same time, so Hunt chose to focus
more on her work behind the camera, only coming

00:03:44.099 --> 00:03:48.629
out of acting "semi-retirement" here and there,
when outside-the-box projects appealed to

00:03:48.629 --> 00:03:49.629
her.

00:03:49.629 --> 00:03:51.180
That makes sense, because ...

00:03:51.180 --> 00:03:53.180
She can afford to be choosy

00:03:53.180 --> 00:03:56.980
Even before her film career blew up, Hunt
slowed her professional pace in the wake of

00:03:56.980 --> 00:04:01.540
Mad About You, and one possible reason may
well be the simplest: she's made a lot of

00:04:01.540 --> 00:04:02.540
money.

00:04:02.540 --> 00:04:05.739
She has absolutely no need to work anymore,
unless she really wants to.

00:04:05.739 --> 00:04:11.249
Hunt's wealth increased magnificently in May
1998, as she and Mad co-star Paul Reiser negotiated

00:04:11.249 --> 00:04:16.950
pay raises from $250,000 an episode to an
incredible $1 million per episode.

00:04:16.950 --> 00:04:21.200
Though this only lasted them through the final
season, those 22 episodes still netted both

00:04:21.200 --> 00:04:23.480
of them $22 million bucks each.

00:04:23.480 --> 00:04:27.950
That, plus Hollywood blockbuster money, has
given her wealth beyond most anyone's wildest

00:04:27.950 --> 00:04:28.950
dreams.

00:04:28.950 --> 00:04:32.030
So the next time you're wondering why Helen
Hunt isn't everywhere anymore, the answer

00:04:32.030 --> 00:04:34.580
might just be: she doesn't have to be.

00:04:34.580 --> 00:04:35.580
Thanks for watching!

